# 4 The inferred decision model

This chapter states, as a catalogue of rules, what Vitis HLS 2025.1 decides between a C/C++ kernel and
the BRAM_18K count of its `csynth` report [@amd2025ug1399], for the configuration of the ground truth (xc7z020clg400-1,
10 ns, `flow_target=vivado`, `syn.array_partition.throughput_driven=off`). Chapter 3 describes how the
rules were found; this chapter describes what was found. Every rule is an inference from black-box
experiments: it is the shortest statement we could find that reproduces every kernel run listed with
it, and it is marked *confirmed* only when standalone kernels reproduce it, *partly confirmed* when part
of the statement (usually its mechanism or trigger) rests on corpus observations or on reading header
or open-source code, and *unconfirmed* when it is an observation without a rule.

The catalogue holds {{n_rules}} rules in {{n_stages}} decision stages, drawn from {{n_notes_cited}} of the
{{n_notes_total}} entries of `hls-doc/notes/VITIS_NOTES.md`, and cites {{n_kernels}} distinct standalone
kernels whose Vitis runs are read back by the generator of this chapter. Of the rules, {{n_confirmed}}
are confirmed, {{n_partly}} partly confirmed and {{n_unconfirmed}} unconfirmed. These counts are per
*rule*: a rule's status is assigned in the rule data from the notes it draws on, and one rule can combine
several notes. Section 3.3 counts the *notes* themselves with the shared heading parser, so its numbers
differ. Entries not cited: {{uncited}}
(N34 records the Phase 6 counting rules for DSP, FF and LUT, which are outside the BRAM model).

## 4.0 How to read a rule

Each rule has the same parts.

- **Statement.** One paragraph in the vocabulary below. Numbers inside a statement are either constants
  of the rule (the 64-trip threshold, the 1024-bit storage threshold, the 16-copy cap, the depth bands)
  or references to a kernel's run, which the generator fills in from that run's `result.json`.
- **Example kernel.** One kernel that shows the rule, with its Vitis total. Appendix A lists, per rule,
  every kernel together with the evidence, the mechanism, the configuration keys and the catalogue effect.
- **Kernels (Appendix A).** The minimal kernels that show the rule, each with what it varies, and, read from its Vitis
  run: the total BRAM_18K, the memory rows (array: banks x words x bits, core, BRAM_18K; cores without the
  `_AUTO` infix), the pipelined loops (trip count, achieved II), and which transformation messages the
  compile log contains (`[HLS 214-376]` automatic pipeline, `[HLS 214-291]` unroll implied by a pipeline,
  `[HLS 214-186]` unroll, `[HLS 214-449]` / `[HLS 214-270]` / `[HLS 214-421]` automatic partitions,
  `[HLS 214-248]` applied partition, `[HLS 200-2061]` / `[XFORM 203-541]` perfect-nest conversion and
  flattening, `[HLS 200-2167]` / `[HLS 200-1457]` RAM_1WNR copies, `[HLS 200-885]` port-limited II,
  `[HLS 214-178]` automatic inlining, `[RTMG 210-278]` / `[RTMG 210-285]` memory and FIFO
  implementation). A kernel that Vitis rejects shows its error message instead.
- **Evidence (Appendix A).** Where in Vitis the behaviour is visible: the report tables, a log message, an IR stage
  (`a.g.ld.5.gdce.ll` after the front end; `a.o.1.ll` .. `a.o.3.ll` after the closed optimisation
  stages), scheduling or binding reports, RTL, or the open-source front end and resource formula.
- **Status**, as above.
- **hls-est.** Whether and how the estimator models the rule (default, opt-in, partly, not), the pass or
  tool that implements it, and the configuration keys through which a designer can change it. Every key
  listed is checked against the default configuration (`hls-est --dump-default-config`) by the generator.
- **Catalogue effect (Appendix A).** Where the change log records the effect of adopting the rule on the full
  HLSFactory catalogue, a verbatim excerpt of that record, with its date and title.

**Notation.** A memory (array or partition bank) has D words of W bits. In a pipelined loop, R and W
count its reads and writes per iteration after unrolling, load merging and invariant hoisting, and II is
the achieved initiation interval. F is a partition factor. A port mode is single port (`ram_1p`), simple
dual port (one write-only and one read-only port, or two read-only ports), two-port (`ram_2p`, a
read-write and a read port) or true dual port (`ram_t2p`). x36 and x18 are the widest data widths of a
RAMB18 in simple-dual-port and in true-dual-port mode.
